WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.219
A two hundred and eighty-four billion parameter model.

00:00:03.219 --> 00:00:04.579
Sixty-four gigabytes of RAM.

00:00:04.579 --> 00:00:05.598
No graphics card.

00:00:05.598 --> 00:00:08.657
So where do the weights that don't fit go?

00:00:11.498 --> 00:00:12.328
Hi, I'm Bexley!

00:00:12.328 --> 00:00:14.264
I expected a trick with the disk.

00:00:14.264 --> 00:00:15.094
There isn't one.

00:00:15.094 --> 00:00:16.201
Let's count the bytes.

00:00:19.925 --> 00:00:20.724
Chapter one.

00:00:20.724 --> 00:00:21.923
Two different sizes.

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:28.927
DeepSeek V4 Flash has forty-three layers.

00:00:28.927 --> 00:00:34.076
In each, a router scores two hundred and fifty-six experts for every token.

00:00:34.496 --> 00:00:35.861
Only the top six run.

00:00:35.861 --> 00:00:39.136
The other two hundred and fifty stay idle, but any of them

00:00:39.136 --> 00:00:40.500
could be picked next token.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:44.829
A shared expert runs for everyone, and attention runs every time too.

00:00:45.249 --> 00:00:47.698
So the whole model has to fit in memory.

00:00:47.698 --> 00:00:50.418
But a token only reads a small slice of it.

00:00:50.418 --> 00:00:51.779
Those are two different numbers.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:56.397
I parsed the header of the default checkpoint.

00:00:56.397 --> 00:01:00.105
Fifty-six point nine three gibibytes, and routed experts are ninety-two point

00:01:00.105 --> 00:01:01.453
seven percent of it.

00:01:01.873 --> 00:01:05.667
One token reads six of two hundred and fifty-six slices per layer.

00:01:05.667 --> 00:01:09.462
That is one point three three gigabytes: a quarter of its reads.

00:01:09.882 --> 00:01:13.438
Attention is a few percent of the file, yet every token reads

00:01:13.438 --> 00:01:15.809
all of it: two point five four gigabytes.

00:01:16.229 --> 00:01:19.792
Add the shared expert and the output head, and one token pulls

00:01:19.792 --> 00:01:22.464
five point three six gigabytes through the memory bus.

00:01:24.383 --> 00:01:27.831
Start with the bytes: five point three six gigabytes per token.

00:01:28.251 --> 00:01:32.122
The README's desktop sustains about seventy-five gigabytes a second.

00:01:32.542 --> 00:01:36.501
Divide, and a token can't take less than seventy-one and a half milliseconds.

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:39.295
That caps decoding at fourteen tokens a second.

00:01:39.715 --> 00:01:42.302
The README measures eight point seven five.

00:01:42.302 --> 00:01:47.106
Sixty-two percent of the ceiling, which is where tuned CPU inference usually lands.

00:01:48.758 --> 00:01:49.329
Chapter two.

00:01:49.329 --> 00:01:51.329
Why not stream the experts from disk?

00:01:55.775 --> 00:02:00.376
The obvious design maps the file and reads experts from an SSD on demand.

00:02:00.376 --> 00:02:04.320
At about seven gigabytes a second, with nothing cached, that is four

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:05.963
point one tokens a second.

00:02:06.383 --> 00:02:07.961
This project does the opposite.

00:02:07.961 --> 00:02:11.749
Most expert weights go to a stock llama.cpp format at about one

00:02:11.749 --> 00:02:15.221
and a half bits, and the whole file stays in RAM.

00:02:16.925 --> 00:02:17.679
Chapter three.

00:02:17.679 --> 00:02:19.188
Three weights, seven bits.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:26.810
A three bit scalar quantizer gives each weight eight levels.

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:30.542
Three weights then sit on a grid of five hundred and twelve

00:02:30.542 --> 00:02:32.096
points, evenly through a cube.

00:02:32.516 --> 00:02:36.372
Vector quantization snaps the triple as a unit, to a codebook of

00:02:36.372 --> 00:02:37.978
a hundred and twenty-eight points.

00:02:37.978 --> 00:02:40.228
That costs seven bits per three weights.

00:02:40.648 --> 00:02:45.339
A block holds sixty-four weights: a scale, twenty seven-bit indices, two short

00:02:45.339 --> 00:02:47.685
tail indices, and eight small sub-scales.

00:02:48.105 --> 00:02:51.814
That is a hundred and ninety-two bits, exactly three bits per weight.

00:02:52.234 --> 00:02:57.028
The whole codebook fits in six vector registers, so one instruction expands

00:02:57.028 --> 00:02:59.824
sixty-four bytes of indices without touching memory.

00:03:01.192 --> 00:03:02.316
Here is the formula.

00:03:03.192 --> 00:03:04.418
d is the block's scale,

00:03:05.849 --> 00:03:07.810
m is the sub-scale for its group of eight,

00:03:08.896 --> 00:03:12.439
and c is one coordinate of the codebook entry its index names.

00:03:13.925 --> 00:03:17.893
And here it runs: the Nemotron thirty B build, on a free

00:03:17.893 --> 00:03:20.869
eight-core cloud instance with thirty-two gigabytes and no GPU.

00:03:21.289 --> 00:03:24.372
The status bar shows an eight K context window.

00:03:24.372 --> 00:03:28.482
Short prompts only: a coding agent's long system prompt did not fit.

00:03:29.983 --> 00:03:31.904
The quality claims need a closer read.

00:03:32.572 --> 00:03:36.947
Ninety-nine point one five percent is one tensor, quantized and restored, from

00:03:36.947 --> 00:03:38.405
the thirty B model.

00:03:38.405 --> 00:03:41.322
It says nothing about the big model's answers.

00:03:41.742 --> 00:03:45.658
The big model never ran at full precision, so its divergence is

00:03:45.658 --> 00:03:47.616
measured against its own default build.

00:03:48.036 --> 00:03:51.932
And on the thirty B, every variant scores a hundred on the gate.

00:03:51.932 --> 00:03:54.030
A test everyone aces cannot rank them.

00:03:55.725 --> 00:03:59.160
The big model fits because its experts are squeezed to about

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:00.410
one point six bits.

00:04:00.410 --> 00:04:03.533
Its speed is set by the layers every token reads.

00:04:04.775 --> 00:04:05.524
To recap.

00:04:05.524 --> 00:04:10.393
A token reads five point three six gigabytes, a quarter of it experts.

00:04:10.393 --> 00:04:11.142
Nothing streams.

00:04:11.142 --> 00:04:14.513
And vector quantization packs three weights into seven bits.

00:04:14.933 --> 00:04:17.052
Every source is in the full article.

00:04:17.052 --> 00:04:17.658
I'm Bexley.

00:04:17.658 --> 00:04:17.961
Bye!
